WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:05.520
To finish this domain on database design,&nbsp;
let's look at backing up and securing our data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:09.760
It is important to make sure a database&nbsp;
is backed up regularly to restore the data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:18.400
if it is accidentally deleted or changed. Ideally,&nbsp;
a backup should go to a different drive than the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:25.360
one hosting a database, but for now, let's just&nbsp;
see how a backup is done. I will select and hold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:32.400
(or right-click) the AdventureWorks database,&nbsp;
select Tasks, and then Back Up. The Back Up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:39.600
Database window appears. We have two choices for&nbsp;
a backup. We can do a full backup, which backs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:48.080
up the entire database, or a differential backup,&nbsp;
which backs up all the changes since the last full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:55.040
backup. If you ever need to restore a database&nbsp;
from a differential backup, you first need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.040 --> 00:01:02.320
restore the last full backup and then the most&nbsp;
recent differential backup of the same database.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:09.440
For this example, we will do a full backup. We&nbsp;
can back this database up to a disk or to a URL,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:16.800
which would most likely be to a storage area&nbsp;
in Azure. I will use a disk for this example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:21.360
I will not run the backup right now, as doing&nbsp;
so would take considerable time, but I wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:26.960
you to see how this is done and emphasize the&nbsp;
importance of backing up a database regularly.

